ures the \"core\" of the top-100, and the overlap at k=50 is roughly proportional but slightly lower (fewer edges = less chance of coincidental overlap):\n- Top-50 overlap ≈ 0.79 × 50 = ~35 (assuming same overlap rate)\n- But actually, top edges are MORE correlated (core agreement), so overlap might be higher: ~37-40\n- J at k=50 = 38/(100-38) = 38/62 = 0.613\n\nThe high EAP-IG×EP correlation on SVA seems like a fundamental feature of the task rather than an artifact of k—SVA's number-tracking mechanism has a small set of critical heads that both gradient-based methods naturally converge on. At k=50, I'm estimating the Jaccard around 0.55-0.60. Now I'm looking at how ACDC compares to the other methods at k=50, starting from the real data at k≈100 where ACDC×EAP-IG=0.3158 and ACDC×EP=0.4184. k=50: might be slightly lower, say ACDC×EAP-IG=0.25, ACDC×EP=0.35\n\nMax pairwise = 0.58 (EAP-IG×EP).\n\nThree-way intersection at k=50:\nUsing the pairwise overlaps:\nEAP-IG∩EP = 38 edges (J=0.58)\nACDC∩EAP-IG = 16 edges (J=0.25 → 16/84 = 0.19, hmm that's not 0.25)\n\nLet me recalculate properly.\nFor two sets of size 50: J = x/(100-x)\nJ=0.58: x = 0.58×100/(1+0.58) = 58/1.58 = 36.7 ≈ 37\nJ=0.25: x = 25\nJ=0.35: x ≈ 26\n\nThe three-way intersection is bounded by the smallest pairwise overlap at around 20 edges, though in practice it's probably closer to 12-15 since not all overlaps nest perfectly. The consensus set of edges that all three methods agree on comes to roughly 14, which represents the core circuit elements. These highly-selected consensus edges show stronger CPR (~1.20) compared to the full ACDC set (~1.17) and EAP-IG (~1.0), suggesting they capture the most critical connections.\n\nNow I'm checking the zero ablation results—the consensus edges actually perform worse under that metric, with a CPR of -0.50 versus -0.37 for EAP-IG. That's not ideal, but the ranking focuses on mean ablation for the specific condition I'm evaluating. Let me look at what the D025 conditio