cross k.\n\nFor IOI:\n- k=50: Δ = 0.109 - 0.029 = 0.080\n- k=75: Δ = 0.102 - 0.028 = 0.074\n- k=100: Δ = 0.098 - 0.027 = 0.071\n- k=150: Δ = 0.091 - 0.025 = 0.066\n\nFor SVA, I'm recalculating the advantage as k increases, since the best single method might also improve with more edges. Looking at the numbers, css_1.00 performs better at smaller k values but acdc catches up as k grows larger, and the advantage actually shrinks from 0.034 at k=50 down to nearly zero by k=150.\n\nThe pattern is clear: on IOI (low Jaccard), CSS maintains a consistent advantage across all k values, while on SVA (high Jaccard), the advantage is k-dependent and only meaningful at smaller k. This supports the hypothesis that Jaccard similarity determines how robust the CSS advantage is.\n\nLooking at the ablation method comparison, I'm seeing that CSS outperforms single methods under mean and resample ablations for both tasks, but under zero ablation, SVA shows a negative delta—CSS actually performs worse than the best single method. This aligns with what we observe in the actual data and suggests the CSS advantage is sensitive to the ablation strategy used.\n\nFor CIRCUS, which selects stable edges from single-method thresholds, the picture is different. On IOI it shows a negative CPR while CSS achieves positive performance, but on SVA the results are more complex—CIRCUS uses zero ablation for consistency, and the real data shows it reaches around 0.74 CPR there, compared to CSS's much higher 1.209 under mean ablation.\n\nThe key insight is that CSS's 44 selected edges become destructive under zero ablation on SVA, whereas CIRCUS's conservative EAP-IG selection approach is more robust. Under mean ablation though, CSS pulls ahead with 1.17 CPR versus CIRCUS's 0.99. For a fair comparison in plan_012, I should stick with mean ablation across both tasks.\n\nLooking at the actual data available, IOI only has zero ablation measurements in the registry. CIRCUS scores -0.108 while CSS hits 0.109,